<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pandya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pandya dynasty was a long-lived Tamil ruling house of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. In the early fourteenth century the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>, serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, sacked the Pandya treasury at Madurai. The campaign was part of the submission of most major southern powers to the sultanate by 1311.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Pandya dynasty" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Borders by Rawn3012 using GIMP · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Pandya_dynasty_at_its_apex.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pandyas, also called the Pandyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, were one of the three great kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>; their rulers were known together as the three crowned rulers, the mu-ventar. The dynasty existed since at least the 5th to 3rd centuries BCE, and its origin and timeline are difficult to establish. Early Pandya chieftains ruled Pandya Nadu, which included the inland city of Madurai and the southern port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korkai">Korkai</a>. Greek accounts, the edicts of the Maurya emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a> coins and inscriptions suggest their continuity from the 3rd century BCE.</p><p>Tamil legend tells that three brothers, Cheran, Cholan and Pandyan, ruled together at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korkai">Korkai</a>, and that the two others then left to found their own kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silappatikaram">Silappatikaram</a> says the Pandya emblem was a fish. Medieval Pandya kings claimed descent from the Lunar Race, and tradition holds that the Sangams, the academies of Tamil poets, were held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> under their patronage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early Pandyas faded into obscurity with the rise of the Kalabhra dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadungon">Kadungon</a> revived the kingdom in the late 6th century, and from the 6th to the 9th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> or the Rashtrakutas, the Pallavas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> and the Pandyas dominated south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, often invading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> country, the Chera country, Venadu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The Pandyas declined with the rise of the Cholas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in the 9th century and were in constant conflict with them.</p><p>They entered a golden age under Maravarman I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I</a> in the 13th century. Jatavarman I, about 1251, expanded the kingdom into the Telugu country as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a>, into south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, and conquered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Maravarman Kulasekhara I defeated an alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> in 1279 and invaded Sri Lanka, from where the Tooth Relic of the Buddha was carried away. Rule was shared among several royals, one holding primacy.</p><p>An internal crisis coincided with the Khalji invasion of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1310-11. Sultanate raids followed, with the loss of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> in 1312, of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in 1323 and the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a> in 1334. Later Pandyas ruled from Tenkasi and Tirunelveli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Early Pandya rulers are traditionally associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and later with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>. Pandya Nadu was home to major temples, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meenakshi-temple">Meenakshi Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, which were large employers and landowners and also served as banks, schools and dispensaries. The Pandyas issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> punch-marked and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins bearing the fish, their emblem. The dynasty is considered one of the longest-ruling in the world, in power from roughly 500 BCE to 1618 CE, though its extent varied greatly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pandya dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cholas were among the Three Crowned Kings of Tamilakam with the Chera and <b>Pandya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He compelled the submission of the Kakatiyas at Warangal (1310), extracted enormous tribute from the Hoysala capital at Dwarasamudra and plundered the <b>Pandya</b> lands around Madurai (1311), so that nearly every major southern Indian power became a tributary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Narasimha II intervened repeatedly to protect the weakened Chola king Rajaraja III against the <b>Pandyas</b> and the Kadava chief Kopperunchinga, and his son Vira Someshvara built a second capital at Kannanur Kuppam near Srirangam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Marco Polo wrote that the horses imported into the <b>Pandya</b> country died quickly in the south Indian climate, so the kings had to buy thousands more each year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The defining conflict of Pallava history was a long struggle with the Chalukyas of Badami for supremacy in the south, fought alongside wars with the <b>Pandyas of Madurai</b>, the Gangas of Talakad and, later, the Rashtrakutas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Govinda III also broke a southern coalition of Pallava, <b>Pandya</b>, Kerala and Ganga rulers and took tribute from Kanchipuram.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
